  {
   "metadata": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "#### 4.1.7 Comparing K-Means and Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering\n",
    "\n",
    "The two clustering methods are compared using the same customer coordinates and several\n",
    "different numbers of depots.\n",
    "\n",
    "$K$-Means directly produces cluster centroids, which makes it straightforward to interpret\n",
    "the centroids as proposed depot locations. It also allows customers to be assigned to the\n",
    "nearest centroid.\n",
    "\n",
    "Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering does not directly produce centroids. Instead, the\n",
    "mean coordinates of the resulting clusters are used as representative locations for\n",
    "comparison.\n",
    "\n",
    "The comparison focuses on the resulting spatial grouping, representative depot\n",
    "locations, and computation time. The results are then used to formulate a recommendation\n",
    "for the placement and number of drone depots."
   ],
   "id": "854fa700dc1a85af"
  },
  {
   "metadata": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": [
    "#### 4.1.8 Depot Placement Recommendation\n",
    "\n",
    "Based on the clustering experiments, increasing the number of depots divides the customer\n",
    "base into smaller service areas and generally places depots closer to groups of customers.\n",
    "However, adding more depots also increases the operational complexity and does not\n",
    "necessarily provide a proportional improvement.\n",
    "\n",
    "For the final recommendation, the company should consider the trade-off between customer\n",
    "coverage, depot locations, clustering quality, and computational cost. The selected number\n",
    "of depots should therefore be based on the observed spatial distribution and the practical\n",
    "requirements of the delivery network."
   ],
   "id": "e554479409f616d5"
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "60f90396ca6043f1",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### 4.2 Finding Interesting Relationships Between Product Groups\n",
    "Association rules were analyzed with the Apriori algorithm implementation from Machine Learning Library Extensions (mlxtend).\n",
    "\n",
    "Product data was prepared to boolean format for this algorithm in chapter 3."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "2e7de4b0d9463219",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 4.2.1 Support\n",
    "\n",
    "Support measures how frequently an item set occurs in the transaction dataset.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, a support value of 0.20 means that the item set occurs in approximately 20% of all transactions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Support is useful for identifying product groups or combinations that occur frequently in customer purchases.\n",
    "\n",
    "A minimum support threshold is used to remove very rare item sets and reduce the number of patterns that need to be analyzed."
   ]
  },
