   "source": [
    "#### 4.2.2 Confidence\n",
    "\n",
    "Confidence measures how often the consequent item set is present when the antecedent item set is present.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, if the rule `{A} -> {B}` has a confidence of 0.80, then approximately 80% of the transactions containing A also contain B.\n",
    "\n",
    "Confidence can therefore be used to identify products that could be recommended after a customer purchases or selects another product group.\n",
    "\n",
    "However, confidence alone does not indicate whether the relationship is genuinely strong. Therefore, lift is also examined."
   ]
  },
