   "id": "0b3d3621-56b3-40db-8d90-98db196350f6",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### 4.2.3 Lift\n",
    "\n",
    "Lift compares the observed frequency of an association rule with the frequency that would be expected if the antecedent and consequent were independent.\n",
    "\n",
    "A lift value greater than 1 indicates a positive association: the consequent occurs more often when the antecedent is present than would be expected from its overall frequency.\n",
    "\n",
    "A lift close to 1 indicates that the two sides of the rule are approximately independent.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, lift is useful for finding product relationships that are stronger than what would be expected simply because the products are individually popular."
   ]
  },
