   "source": [
    "#### 4.2.8 Final Recommendation\n",
    "\n",
    "The association rule mining results can be used as a data-driven product recommendation system.\n",
    "\n",
    "First, products and itemsets with high support can be used for inventory planning because they occur frequently in customer transactions.\n",
    "\n",
    "Second, high-confidence rules can be used to generate \"Also buy\" recommendations. When a customer purchases the products in the antecedent, the company can recommend the products in the consequent.\n",
    "\n",
    "Third, lift can be used to identify relationships that are stronger than would be expected from the general popularity of the products. High-lift rules are therefore useful candidates for targeted cross-selling and product bundling.\n",
    "\n",
    "The company should combine support, confidence and lift rather than relying on one metric. A useful recommendation rule should occur often enough to be operationally relevant, have sufficiently high confidence, and preferably have a lift clearly above 1.\n",
    "\n",
    "For example, the observed rules involving product groups such as Prod2, Prod15 and Prod9 show that some product combinations have both high confidence and substantially higher lift than 1. Such relationships can be tested as \"also buy\" recommendations or product bundles.\n",
    "\n",
    "The recommendations should be evaluated after deployment by measuring whether they increase additional purchases and average transaction value. Association rules describe relationships in historical transactions and should therefore not be interpreted as proof that purchasing one product causes the purchase of another."
   ]
  },
