   "source": [
    "### 5.1 Depot Placement Analysis and Recommendation\n",
    "\n",
    "The K-Means analysis provides explicit candidate depot coordinates because each cluster centroid represents the centre of its customer group.\n",
    "\n",
    "The required three-depot configuration provides a simple starting solution. When more depots are introduced, the customer groups become smaller and the centroids generally move closer to local customer concentrations. This reduces K-Means inertia, but additional depots also increase the number of physical locations that the company must operate.\n",
    "\n",
    "The computation-time experiments show how the clustering cost changes with the number of depots. Small variations between individual runs are expected because computation time depends on the execution environment.\n",
    "\n",
    "Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering provides a different way of grouping customers. Unlike K-Means, it does not directly produce centroids, so the mean coordinates of the resulting clusters were used as representative locations.\n",
    "\n",
    "K-Means is particularly practical for this depot-location problem because the centroids directly provide candidate depot coordinates and customers can naturally be assigned to their nearest centroid.\n",
    "\n",
    "The company should therefore select the final number of depots by considering the trade-off between customer coverage, delivery distance, operational cost and depot capacity. The clustering results provide candidate configurations rather than a final business decision by themselves."
   ]
  },
