   "source": [
    "### 5.2 Association Rule Mining Result Analysis and Recommendations\n",
    "\n",
    "The association rule mining results provide three complementary types of information.\n",
    "\n",
    "Support identifies products and itemsets that occur frequently in the transaction data. These results can be used for inventory planning and to ensure that frequently purchased product groups are available.\n",
    "\n",
    "Confidence identifies product relationships that can be used for direct recommendations. If customers frequently purchase the consequent when the antecedent is present, the consequent can be presented as an \"Also buy\" recommendation.\n",
    "\n",
    "Lift identifies relationships that are stronger than would be expected from the individual popularity of the products. Rules with lift greater than 1 indicate positive associations and can be considered for targeted cross-selling and product bundling.\n",
    "\n",
    "The company should combine all three metrics when selecting rules for deployment. A rule with high confidence but very low support may have limited business value, while a rule with high support but lift close to 1 may provide little additional information beyond general product popularity.\n",
    "\n",
    "The recommended approach is therefore to use high-support itemsets for inventory planning and to use rules with suitable support, confidence and lift for personalised product recommendations.\n",
    "\n",
    "After deployment, the company should measure recommendation click-through, additional purchases, conversion rate and average transaction value to determine whether the association rules produce measurable revenue improvements."
   ]
